// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0+
/*
 * Radxa ROCK 4B+ (RK3399 / OP1) additions to the U-Boot device tree.
 *
 * WHY THIS FILE HAS TO EXIST
 *
 * U-Boot looks for a file of exactly this name and silently does without it:
 *
 *   scripts/Makefile.lib:200
 *     u_boot_dtsi_options = $(strip $(wildcard $(u_boot_dtsi_loc)$(basename $(notdir $<))-u-boot.dtsi) ...)
 *
 * The basename of rk3399-rock-4b-plus.dts is matched against
 * arch/arm/dts/rk3399-rock-4b-plus-u-boot.dtsi.  No file with that name exists,
 * so the first match falls through to the generic rk3399-u-boot.dtsi, and the
 * board .dtb ends up as the board .dts plus that generic layer -- which is
 * enough to satisfy binman and to mark nodes for the SPL, but not enough to
 * initialise DRAM.
 *
 * rk3399-u-boot.dtsi and its rockchip-u-boot.dtsi do not carry
 * rockchip,sdram-params.  The TPL therefore reaches
 * drivers/ram/rockchip/sdram_rk3399.c:rk3399_dmc_of_to_plat(), dev_read_u32_array()
 * returns FDT_ERR_NOTFOUND (-1), and the board stops before the SPL ever runs:
 *
 *   U-Boot TPL 2025.10-OpenWrt-r33051-f5dae5ece4 (Jun 29 2026 - 12:59:20)
 *   rk3399_dmc_of_to_plat: Cannot read rockchip,sdram-params -1
 *   DRAM init failed: -1
 *   Trying to boot from BOOTROM
 *
 * Nothing in the build says so.  There is no warning, no failing target, and the
 * resulting idbloader.img is not distinguishable from a working one by size --
 * the failure only shows up if that bootloader is put on the SPI, at which point
 * the board is bricked with no way to write a different one.
 *
 * Every other RK3399 board in U-Boot has one of these files: rock-pi-4a,
 * rock-pi-4c, rock-4c-plus, rock-4se, nanopc-t4.  That is why those boot and
 * this one did not.  The difference is visible in the compiled .dtb: adding the
 * parameters takes this board's from 90840 to 93704 bytes, and
 * rockchip,sdram-params is the only thing in it that changes.  A nanopc-t4
 * build of the same tree shows the same property and grows by 5720 instead,
 * because its blob is LPDDR3.
 *
 * WHAT IS INCLUDED, AND WHY ONLY THESE TWO
 *
 * Two includes, matching the shape of every other rk3399 board in U-Boot:
 *
 *   rk3399-u-boot.dtsi
 *       Carries the binman node -- CONFIG_BINMAN is enabled, and without a
 *       'binman' node in the control FDT the build stops at
 *       "binman: Device tree './u-boot.dtb' does not have a 'binman' node" --
 *       and the bootph-all / bootph-pre-ram marks that keep nodes in the SPL and
 *       TPL device trees.
 *
 *       It has to be included here explicitly, and that is the subtle part.
 *       scripts/Makefile.lib builds a priority chain, not a union:
 *
 *         u_boot_dtsi_options = $(strip $(wildcard <board>-u-boot.dtsi) \
 *                                $(wildcard $(CONFIG_SYS_SOC)-u-boot.dtsi) ...)
 *         # We use the first match to be included
 *         dtsi_include_list = $(notdir $(firstword $(u_boot_dtsi_options)))
 *
 *       So with no file named after this board, the first match is
 *       $(CONFIG_SYS_SOC)-u-boot.dtsi, i.e. rk3399-u-boot.dtsi, which is why
 *       binman was satisfied before this file existed -- and why the missing
 *       piece was only the DRAM parameters.
 *
 *       Adding this file makes it the first match, and rk3399-u-boot.dtsi then
 *       stops being included at all.  Creating the file without reproducing the
 *       chain inside it moves the failure from runtime (a bricked board) to
 *       build time (binman).  Include it, as nanopc-t4 does through
 *       rk3399-nanopc-t4-u-boot.dtsi -> rk3399-nanopi4-u-boot.dtsi -> here.
 *       DEVICE_TREE_DEBUG=y makes the build print which candidate matched.
 *
 *   rk3399-sdram-lpddr4-100.dtsi
 *       Nothing but &dmc { rockchip,sdram-params = <...> }, which is the property
 *       the TPL fails on.  The board has 64-bit dual-channel LPDDR4 at 3200 Mb/s
 *       (2 or 4 GB), and mainline U-Boot uses this same file for every RK3399
 *       board with that memory: rk3399-rock-pi-4-u-boot.dtsi includes exactly
 *       this one, and rock-pi-4a, rock-pi-4c, rock-4c-plus and rock-4se include
 *       that in turn.
 *
 * NOT INCLUDED: rk3399-rock-pi-4-u-boot.dtsi, which would be the obvious
 * one-liner, also pulls in &sdhci timing overrides (mmc-ddr-1_8v,
 * mmc-hs200-1_8v) and an leds node.  Those are defensible but they are not what
 * is missing here, and the eMMC already reaches HS400 without them.  On a board
 * that currently cannot boot, only the two things the build and the TPL actually
 * ask for should change.
 *
 * No spi1/flash@0 node is added either.  SPI boot on this board goes through
 * CONFIG_ROCKCHIP_SPI_IMAGE rather than the MMC-style flash node lookup, and the
 * working nanopc-t4 .dtb contains no flash@0 node either.
 */

#include "rk3399-u-boot.dtsi"
#include "rk3399-sdram-lpddr4-100.dtsi"
